      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": "HB3rDPj12k6yg9MFclavC",
      "index": "bVtl",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 855417014,
      "version": 2399,
      "versionNonce": 9485023,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1770820903807,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- As per the core entities, we can see the schema \nis structured.\n- We need high consistency for matches/swipes\n- There are defined relationships between the \nentities.\n- Considering 20M daily Active Users\n- 100 swipes per day per user\n- 100 * 20 * 10^6 = 2 billion swipes per day\n- 2 * 10^9 / 80 * 10 ^ 3 (number of seconds in a day)\n    = 200K requests per second\n    Even if we make it 1000 RPS in peak hours\n- POSTGRESQL can handle 5K-10K TPS easily. [THIS IS\nSOMTHING WE HAVE TO DEEP DIVE]\n\n- Each swipe is 100 bytes per row.\n- 2 billion * 100 bytes per row\n= 200 GB per day\n= per year ~  In the scale of Peta bytes\n\n- Considering the number of transactions and the data\nwe have to make database selection properly.\n\n- We started with POSTGRESQL, but after looking at \nthe TPS and data size, we should rethink our choice.\n\n- We will revsisit this in our detail design section.\n\n",
      "fontSize": 14.56304931640625,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- As per the core entities, we can see the schema \nis structured.\n- We need high consistency for matches/swipes\n- There are defined relationships between the \nentities.\n- Considering 20M daily Active Users\n- 100 swipes per day per user\n- 100 * 20 * 10^6 = 2 billion swipes per day\n- 2 * 10^9 / 80 * 10 ^ 3 (number of seconds in a day)\n    = 200K requests per second\n    Even if we make it 1000 RPS in peak hours\n- POSTGRESQL can handle 5K-10K TPS easily. [THIS IS\nSOMTHING WE HAVE TO DEEP DIVE]\n\n- Each swipe is 100 bytes per row.\n- 2 billion * 100 bytes per row\n= 200 GB per day\n= per year ~  In the scale of Peta bytes\n\n- Considering the number of transactions and the data\nwe have to make database selection properly.\n\n- We started with POSTGRESQL, but after looking at \nthe TPS and data size, we should rethink our choice.\n\n- We will revsisit this in our detail design section.\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "HB3rDPj12k6yg9MFclavC",
